---A kind of block, for `rex.block.call`.
---@alias rex.Creator string
---| "com.superlogical.onboarding"
---| "com.superlogical.terminal"

---What to create in a layout, for `rex.layout.block{ flavor = ... }`.
---@alias rex.Flavor string
---| "com.superlogical.onboarding.onboarding"
---| "com.superlogical.terminal.shell"

---Session methods. `session_id` is filled in from the current session when it
---is left out; other IDs, such as `block_id`, are not. Every call returns a
---result table, or `nil` and an error message — a failure never raises.
---
---```lua
---local view, err = rex.session.view{}
---```
---@class rex.SessionAPI
local SessionAPI = {}

---@class rex.session.view.args
---@field session_id? string The session to act on.

---@class rex.session.view.result.windows.item.layers.item.blocks.item
---@field block_id? string The block's ID.
---@field creator_name? string The creator that made the block, such as com.superlogical.terminal.
---@field flavor? string The flavor the block was made from, such as com.superlogical.terminal.shell.
---@field host_id? string Identifies the machine and account the block runs as. If it equals your local server's host_id, the block sees the same files you do. Otherwise treat the block as remote.
---@field label? string The block's label.
---@field owner? string The client that owns the block. Left out when the block has no owner.

---How this node divides in two. Set only when block_id isn't.
---@class rex.ViewLayoutNode.split
---@field after rex.ViewLayoutNode The node on the right or bottom.
---@field before rex.ViewLayoutNode The node on the left or top.
---@field direction "horizontal"|"vertical" How the space is divided: 'horizontal' puts before on the left and after on the right, and 'vertical' puts before above after. Draw a direction you don't know as 'horizontal'.
---@field ratio number The fraction of the space that before gets, between 0 and 1.
---@field split_id? string The split's ID. It stays the same while the split exists, so you can match splits across views.

---How the layer divides its space between blocks, as a tree of splits. Null when the layer is empty.
---@class rex.ViewLayoutNode
---@field block_id? string The block at this node. Set only when split isn't.
---@field rect number[] Where this node sits, as [x, y, w, h] fractions of the window's width and height.
---@field split? rex.ViewLayoutNode.split How this node divides in two. Set only when block_id isn't.

---@class rex.session.view.result.windows.item.layers.item
---@field blocks? rex.session.view.result.windows.item.layers.item.blocks.item[] The blocks in the layer, in layout order. The layout field says where each one sits.
---@field bounds number[] Where the layer sits, as [x, y, w, h] fractions of the window's width and height.
---@field kind string How the layer is placed: 'tiled' for the layer that fills the window, or 'floating' for a floating layer. Draw a kind you don't know as 'floating'.
---@field layer_id? string The layer's ID.
---@field layout? rex.ViewLayoutNode How the layer divides its space between blocks, as a tree of splits. Null when the layer is empty.

---@class rex.session.view.result.windows.item
---@field focused_block_id? string The window's focused block, which receives typing while the window is active. Left out if the window has none.
---@field label? string The window's label.
---@field layers? rex.session.view.result.windows.item.layers.item[] The window's layers, from back to front. The first is the tiled layer, which holds the window's layout and fills the window.
---@field window_id? string The window's ID.
---@field zoomed_block_id? string The block zoomed to fill the window, if any. Layouts keep their normal sizes, so draw this block over the whole window.

---@class rex.session.view.result
---@field active_window_id? string The window the session shows. Left out when the session has no windows.
---@field label? string The session's label.
---@field revision integer The view's revision. It goes up by one with every change, so a larger revision is a newer view of the session.
---@field session_id? string The session's ID.
---@field windows? rex.session.view.result.windows.item[] The session's windows, in order. Rex.app shows them as tabs in this order.

---Get the session's windows, their layouts, and their blocks in one snapshot.
---
---@see rex.Method # `rex api describe session.view` documents every argument.
---@param args? rex.session.view.args
---@return rex.session.view.result|nil result
---@return string|nil err
function SessionAPI.view(args) end

---@class rex.session.attach.args
---@field session_id? string The session to attach to.

---The session's view, read after the connection started receiving its events. Apply it first, then the events whose seq is higher than the response's.
---@class rex.session.attach.result.view
---@field active_window_id? string The window the session shows. Left out when the session has no windows.
---@field label? string The session's label.
---@field revision integer The view's revision. It goes up by one with every change, so a larger revision is a newer view of the session.
---@field session_id? string The session's ID.
---@field windows? rex.session.view.result.windows.item[] The session's windows, in order. Rex.app shows them as tabs in this order.

---@class rex.session.attach.result
---@field client_id string This connection's client ID. It's the same for every session the connection attaches to.
---@field view rex.session.attach.result.view The session's view, read after the connection started receiving its events. Apply it first, then the events whose seq is higher than the response's.

---Attach to a session: start receiving its events and get its current view.
---
---@see rex.Method # `rex api describe session.attach` documents every argument.
---@param args? rex.session.attach.args
---@return rex.session.attach.result|nil result
---@return string|nil err
function SessionAPI.attach(args) end

---@class rex.session.attach_all.args
---@field event_scope string Which events to receive. 'session' sends every session's events except block_event, plus session_created, session_destroyed, session_moved, and tailscale_status_changed. 'all' also sends block_event and the client events, such as client_joined.

---@class rex.session.attach_all.result.sessions.item
---@field label string The session's label.
---@field session_id string The session's ID.

---@class rex.session.attach_all.result
---@field sessions? rex.session.attach_all.result.sessions.item[] Every session on the server when this connection started receiving events.

---Start receiving events from the whole server and get the list of sessions.
---
---*Acts on the server, not on one session. `rex.call("session.attach_all", ...)` reads better.*
---
---@see rex.Method # `rex api describe session.attach_all` documents every argument.
---@param args rex.session.attach_all.args
---@return rex.session.attach_all.result|nil result
---@return string|nil err
function SessionAPI.attach_all(args) end

---@class rex.session.bootstrap.args.data_tickets.item
---@field creator_name string Issue tickets for blocks whose creator_name is this, such as com.superlogical.terminal. Required.
---@field data_ref_id string Which of each block's data sources the ticket opens, such as terminal.
---@field subprotocol? string The subprotocol the data connection will use, such as rex.data.terminal.v1. If set, each ticket works only on a connection that uses it.

---@class rex.session.bootstrap.args
---@field attach_session_ids? string[] The sessions to attach to, in order. Unknown and repeated IDs are skipped, and an empty list attaches none. Leave it out to let the server pick one.
---@field data_tickets? rex.session.bootstrap.args.data_tickets.item[] The kinds of block to issue data tickets for. The response has a ticket for each visible block in an attached session that matches one. At most 8 entries.
---@field event_scope string Which events to receive. 'session' sends every session's events except block_event, plus session_created, session_destroyed, session_moved, and tailscale_status_changed. 'all' also sends block_event and the client events, such as client_joined.
---@field preferred_session_id? string The session to attach to, if it exists. Otherwise the server attaches the first session. Ignored when attach_session_ids is set.

---What session.attach_all returns: every session on the server when this connection started receiving events.
---@class rex.session.bootstrap.result.attach_all
---@field sessions? rex.session.attach_all.result.sessions.item[] Every session on the server when this connection started receiving events.

---The session's entry from attach_all.
---@class rex.session.bootstrap.result.attached_sessions.item.session
---@field label string The session's label.
---@field session_id string The session's ID.

---@class rex.session.bootstrap.result.attached_sessions.item
---@field session rex.session.bootstrap.result.attached_sessions.item.session The session's entry from attach_all.
---@field view rex.session.attach.result.view The session's view, read after the connection started receiving its events. Apply it first, then the events whose seq is higher than the response's.

---@class rex.session.bootstrap.result.data_tickets.item
---@field block_id string The block the ticket opens.
---@field data_ref_id string The data source the ticket opens.
---@field expires_in_ms integer How long the ticket stays valid after the server issues it, in milliseconds.
---@field session_id string The session that holds the block.
---@field subprotocol? string The subprotocol the ticket is bound to. Left out if the request named none.
---@field ticket string A single-use ticket to send in the HELLO message that opens the data connection. Only a data connection from the same user can use it.

---The session the server chose and attached. Null if you sent attach_session_ids or there are no sessions.
---@class rex.session.bootstrap.result.selected_session
---@field session rex.session.bootstrap.result.attached_sessions.item.session The session's entry from attach_all.
---@field view rex.session.attach.result.view The session's view, read after the connection started receiving its events. Apply it first, then the events whose seq is higher than the response's.

---The configuration file the server loaded most recently. Always present, even when there is no file.
---@class rex.session.bootstrap.result.server_status.config
---@field generation integer How many times the server has loaded its configuration file since it started, counting from 1. It goes up on every load, even one that fails.
---@field hash string The SHA-256 of the configuration file and the modules it requires while it loads, in lowercase hexadecimal. Empty if there's no file or it couldn't be read.

---The Tailscale account that signed the device in. Left out until Tailscale reports it.
---@class rex.session.bootstrap.result.server_status.tailscale.account
---@field login_name string The Tailscale login name, usually an email address. For display only, because it can change.

---The state of the device's HTTPS listener. Left out while built-in Tailscale is off, and by older servers.
---@class rex.session.bootstrap.result.server_status.tailscale.https
---@field state string 'pending' until the HTTPS listener is ready, then 'available'. The server is reachable over the tailnet only when available. Treat a value you don't know as 'pending'.
---@field url? string The server's address on the tailnet, such as https://rex.tail1234.ts.net. Present once HTTPS is available and the device's name is known.

---The status of built-in Tailscale. Present only when the server can run it.
---@class rex.session.bootstrap.result.server_status.tailscale
---@field account? rex.session.bootstrap.result.server_status.tailscale.account The Tailscale account that signed the device in. Left out until Tailscale reports it.
---@field allow_tailnet_members boolean True if anyone your tailnet's access rules allow may connect. False lets in only the Tailscale user who signed the device in.
---@field auth_url? string The link to open to sign the device in to Tailscale. Present while state is needs_login, once Tailscale has made the link.
---@field dns_name? string The device's MagicDNS name, such as rex.tail1234.ts.net. Left out until known.
---@field error? string Why built-in Tailscale turned off after its HTTPS listener failed. The next enable clears it.
---@field https? rex.session.bootstrap.result.server_status.tailscale.https The state of the device's HTTPS listener. Left out while built-in Tailscale is off, and by older servers.
---@field state string 'disabled' (off), 'starting', 'needs_login' (see auth_url), 'needs_machine_approval' (a tailnet admin must approve the device), or 'running' (on the tailnet). Treat a value you don't know as 'starting'.
---@field tags? string[] The Tailscale tags the device has, such as tag:rex. A tagged device has no owner. Left out when it has none.
---@field tailnet_name? string The tailnet's display name, or its domain if it has none. Left out until known.

---The server's status, as server.status returns it.
---@class rex.session.bootstrap.result.server_status
---@field api_min_revision? integer The oldest revision of the API the server supports. Left out when it is 0.
---@field api_revision? integer The newest revision of the API the server supports. Left out when it is 0.
---@field config rex.session.bootstrap.result.server_status.config The configuration file the server loaded most recently. Always present, even when there is no file.
---@field exit_empty boolean True if the server stops on its own soon after its last session and last connection close.
---@field host_id? string Identifies the machine and account the server runs as. Servers that see the same files report the same value. Left out when the server has no state directory.
---@field instance_id string A random ID for this run of the server. A new value means a new server process, even if pid is the same.
---@field pid integer The server's process ID.
---@field session_count integer How many sessions the server has.
---@field started_at string When the server started, as an RFC 3339 timestamp.
---@field tailscale? rex.session.bootstrap.result.server_status.tailscale The status of built-in Tailscale. Present only when the server can run it.

---@class rex.session.bootstrap.result
---@field attach_all rex.session.bootstrap.result.attach_all What session.attach_all returns: every session on the server when this connection started receiving events.
