---@class rex.onboarding.set_step.args
---@field step string The step to move to. Empty means the first step.

---@class rex.onboarding.set_step.result
---@field generation integer Increases with every change to the state.
---@field step? string The step the walkthrough is on. Empty means the first step.

---@class rex.terminal.blob_read.args
---@field id? integer The result to read, from the reply or event that named it, such as the id in a format reply or a selection_copied event. Zero or left out reads the newest result, whatever produced it. If the server no longer has the result, the call fails with not_found.
---@field max_bytes? integer The most bytes of data to return, measured once it is encoded as a JSON string. Zero or left out means 49152 (48 KiB), and values above 983040 (960 KiB) are lowered to that. Must not be negative.
---@field offset? integer Where to start reading, in bytes from the start of the result. To read the next part, add the length in bytes of the data you received. Must not be negative.

---@class rex.terminal.blob_read.result
---@field data string The text read, starting at offset. It never ends partway through a character. It can be shorter than max_bytes even when more text remains.
---@field eof boolean True if data reaches the end of the result.
---@field id integer The result that was read. Send it in later requests so that every part comes from the same result.
---@field total integer The length of the whole result, in bytes.

---@class rex.terminal.clipboard_read.args
---@field id? integer The copy to read, from a clipboard_written event or an earlier reply. Zero or left out reads the newest copy. If the server no longer has the copy, the call fails with not_found.
---@field max_bytes? integer The most bytes of data to return, measured once it is encoded as a JSON string. Zero or left out means 49152 (48 KiB), and values above 983040 (960 KiB) are lowered to that. Must not be negative.
---@field offset? integer Where to start reading, in bytes from the start of the text. To read the next part, add the length in bytes of the data you received. Must not be negative.

---@class rex.terminal.clipboard_read.result
---@field data string The text read, starting at offset. It never ends partway through a character. It can be shorter than max_bytes even when more text remains.
---@field eof boolean True if data reaches the end of the text.
---@field id integer The copy that was read. Send it in later requests so that every part comes from the same copy.
---@field total integer The length of the whole copied text, in bytes.

---@class rex.terminal.format.args
---@field extra_charsets? boolean Include the selected character sets. Only used with the vt format.
---@field extra_cursor? boolean Include the cursor position. Only used with the vt format.
---@field extra_hyperlink? boolean Include the hyperlink in effect at the cursor. Only used with the vt format.
---@field extra_keyboard? boolean Include keyboard modes such as modifyOtherKeys. Only used with the vt format.
---@field extra_kitty_keyboard? boolean Include the state of the Kitty keyboard protocol. Only used with the vt format.
---@field extra_modes? boolean Include terminal modes that differ from their defaults. Only used with the vt format.
---@field extra_palette? boolean Include the color palette. Only used with the vt format.
---@field extra_protection? boolean Include the character protection mode. Only used with the vt format.
---@field extra_pwd? boolean Include the reported working directory. Only used with the vt format.
---@field extra_scrolling_region? boolean Include the scrolling region. Only used with the vt format.
---@field extra_style? boolean Include the text style in effect at the cursor. Only used with the vt format.
---@field extra_tabstops? boolean Include the tab stops. Only used with the vt format.
---@field format? string The format to return: 'text' for plain text, 'html' for HTML with inline styles, or 'vt' for escape sequences that redraw the screen with its colors and styles. Defaults to 'text'.
---@field max_bytes? integer The most bytes of content to return, measured once it is encoded as a JSON string. If more output remains, the reply has an id, and you read the rest with blob_read. Values above 983040 (960 KiB) are lowered to that. Zero or left out returns the whole output in one reply, and the call fails with too_large if the output doesn't fit. Must not be negative.
---@field trim? boolean Remove spaces from the end of each line.
---@field unwrap? boolean Join lines that the terminal wrapped because they were too long, so each line of output is one line the program wrote.

---@class rex.terminal.format.result
---@field content string The screen and scrollback in the requested format. If id is set, this is only the first part.
---@field eof? boolean True if content is the whole output. Sent only when the request set max_bytes. Older servers never send it, so check id instead to tell whether more output remains.
---@field id? integer Set only when more output remains. To read the rest, call blob_read with this id and with offset set to the length of content in bytes. A reply without id holds the whole output.
---@field total? integer The length of the whole output in bytes. Sent only when the request set max_bytes, and left out when it is 0.

---@class rex.terminal.input_mode.result
---@field canonical boolean True if input reaches the program a line at a time, with backspace and similar keys handled before the program sees it.
---@field echo boolean True if typed characters appear on the screen. A program usually turns this off while it reads a password.
---@field signals boolean True if keys such as Ctrl+C and Ctrl+Z send signals to the foreground process instead of reaching it as input.

---@class rex.terminal.list_dir.args
---@field files? boolean Include entries that aren't directories, such as files, and mark each with file set to true.
---@field limit? integer The most entries to return. Zero, negative, or left out means 2000.
---@field path? string The directory to list: an absolute path, ~ or a path under ~/ for the home directory, or a path relative to the working directory of the terminal's foreground process. Empty lists that working directory.
---@field prefix? string Keep only entries whose names start with this text, ignoring case. Use it to narrow a listing that came back truncated.

---@class rex.terminal.list_dir.result.entries.item
---@field file? boolean True if the entry isn't a directory, or is a link to something that isn't. Only set when the request sets files.
---@field name string The entry's name within the directory.
---@field symlink? boolean True if the entry is a symbolic link. A link that leads nowhere is left out of the listing.

---@class rex.terminal.list_dir.result
---@field entries? rex.terminal.list_dir.result.entries.item[] The matching entries, hidden ones included, in the order the filesystem returns them. Sorting and hiding are up to the client. Empty unless status is 'ok'.
---@field home string The home directory of the terminal's program. Empty if the server can't tell.
---@field path string The directory that was listed, as an absolute path. It is set even when the directory couldn't be read.
---@field status string Whether the directory could be read: 'ok', 'missing' (it doesn't exist), 'not_dir' (it, or a directory on its path, isn't a directory), or 'denied' (the server isn't allowed to read it).
---@field truncated boolean True if some matching entries were left out because the listing reached limit or grew too large for one reply. To see the rest, list again with a prefix.

---@class rex.terminal.modes.result.modes.item
---@field ansi? boolean True for an ANSI mode, which a program sets with ESC [ number h. Left out for a DEC private mode, which a program sets with ESC [ ? number h. The two kinds are numbered separately, so the same number can name one mode of each kind.
---@field default? boolean True if the mode is on in a new terminal and after a reset. Left out if it is off. A mode whose set differs from its default was changed by a program.
---@field name string The mode's name, as Ghostty names it, such as 'bracketed_paste' or 'alt_screen_save_cursor_clear_enter'.
---@field number integer The number a program sends to set or reset the mode, such as 2004 for bracketed paste.
---@field set boolean True if the mode is on.

---@class rex.terminal.modes.result
---@field modes? rex.terminal.modes.result.modes.item[] Every mode the terminal tracks, ANSI modes first, each group by number. Newer servers can add modes, so look modes up by name or number and skip any you don't know.
---@field screen string Which screen the terminal shows: 'primary', or 'alternate' while a full-screen program such as an editor has switched to it with mode 47, 1047, or 1049. Treat any other value as 'primary'.

---@class rex.ProcessInfo
---@field argv0? string The first argument as the process shows it now, such as -zsh for a login shell, cut to 256 bytes. Rex never returns the other arguments, because they can hold secrets.
---@field cwd? string The process's working directory, as an absolute path. Left out if it can't be read, as for another user's process.
---@field invoked_path? string The absolute path the executable was started through, before symlinks were resolved, such as a link in a bin directory. Present only when Rex can confirm it leads to the same file as path.
---@field name string The file name of the executable, such as zsh. When path is left out, the system's own name for the process, which Linux limits to 15 bytes. To label a process, use the first of script, argv0, and name that is present.
---@field path? string The absolute path of the executable, with symlinks resolved. Left out if it can't be read, as for another user's process on Linux.
---@field pid integer The process ID.
---@field script? string For an interpreter such as node or python, the absolute path of the script it is running, as the user named it. Left out for other programs.
---@field user? string The name of the account the process runs as. Left out if it can't be looked up.
---@field user_id integer The user ID the process runs as on Unix, which shows when a command runs as another user, such as with sudo. Zero on Windows.

---How the terminal's program exited. Null while it is still running, and for a terminal that didn't start a program of its own.
---@class rex.terminal.process.result.last_exit
---@field error? string Why the program failed, such as 'exit status 1'. Left out when it exited successfully.
---@field exit_code integer The program's exit code, or -1 if it has none, as when a signal ended it.

---@class rex.terminal.process.result
---@field ancestors? rex.ProcessInfo[] The processes between foreground and child, nearest first: the parent of foreground, then its parent, and so on, ending with child. Left out when foreground is child, when there are more than 16, or when one of them has exited.
---@field child? rex.ProcessInfo The process the terminal started, usually a shell. On macOS a login shell runs under login, and this describes login. Left out if there is no such process or it can't be read.
---@field foreground? rex.ProcessInfo The process in the foreground, such as the shell at its prompt or the command it is running. For a wrapper such as sudo, git, or cargo, it is the command the wrapper runs. Left out if it can't be found or read.
---@field home? string The home directory that the terminal's program inherited, for showing and expanding ~ in paths.
---@field last_exit? rex.terminal.process.result.last_exit How the terminal's program exited. Null while it is still running, and for a terminal that didn't start a program of its own.

---The terminal's foreground process when the report arrived. Left out if the server couldn't tell.
---@class rex.terminal.program_status.result.records.item.owner
---@field name string The process's name, found the same way as name in the result of process. Compare it as well as pid, because the system reuses process IDs.
---@field pid integer The process ID.

---@class rex.terminal.program_status.result.records.item
---@field app? string The program's name, such as cargo or terraform, exactly as it reported it.
---@field effective_app? string The name of the program the record belongs to: app if it is set, or else the app of the nearest parent record that has one. Left out if no record in the chain has an app.
---@field id? string The record's ID. A slash separates a parent from a child, as in build/test. Left out for the root record, which holds the reports that give no ID.
---@field kind? string What a blocked program is waiting for: 'permission', 'question', or 'auth' (a password or other credential). Left out if the program didn't say. Treat a value you don't know as if it were left out.
---@field msg? string One line of text describing the status. Show it as plain text. A client may shorten it but shouldn't try to interpret it.
---@field owner? rex.terminal.program_status.result.records.item.owner The terminal's foreground process when the report arrived. Left out if the server couldn't tell.
---@field progress? integer How far along the program is, as a percentage from 0 to 100. Only working and blocked records have one, and only if the program reported it.
---@field state string What the program says it is doing: 'idle', 'working', 'done', 'blocked' (waiting for the user), or 'error'. Treat a value you don't know as 'idle'.
---@field title? string A short label for the record, such as the name of a task. It comes from the program, so show it as plain text.
---@field updated_at string When the report arrived, by the server's clock, as an RFC 3339 timestamp.

---@class rex.terminal.program_status.result
---@field records rex.terminal.program_status.result.records.item[] Every record, sorted by id. The root record comes first, and each parent comes before its children. Empty if no program has reported a status.

---The process that reported the directory. Left out if pwd is left out or the server couldn't tell.
---@class rex.terminal.pwd.result.owner
---@field name string The process's name, found the same way as name in the result of process. Compare it as well as pid, because the system reuses process IDs.
---@field pid integer The process ID.

---@class rex.terminal.pwd.result
---@field owner? rex.terminal.pwd.result.owner The process that reported the directory. Left out if pwd is left out or the server couldn't tell.
---@field pwd? string The reported directory: a file:// URL when the program used OSC 7, or a plain path otherwise. Left out if no directory has been reported or a reset cleared it.

---@class rex.terminal.resize.args
---@field cell_height_px? integer The height of one character cell in pixels, from 1 to 65535. Send it together with cell_width_px, or leave both out to keep the current cell size.
---@field cell_width_px? integer The width of one character cell in pixels, from 1 to 65535. Send it together with cell_height_px, or leave both out to keep the current cell size.
---@field columns integer The width in columns, that is, characters per line, from 1 to 65535.
---@field rows integer The height in rows, from 1 to 65535.

---@class rex.terminal.set_theme.args
---@field background? string The default background color, as hex RRGGBB or #RRGGBB, such as #011627. Empty or left out uses the built-in color.
---@field cursor? string The cursor color, as hex RRGGBB or #RRGGBB, such as #80a4c2. Empty or left out uses the built-in color.
---@field foreground? string The default text color, as hex RRGGBB or #RRGGBB, such as #d6deeb. Empty or left out uses the built-in color.
---@field palette? string[] Changes to the 256-color palette, each written as index=RRGGBB or index=#RRGGBB with an index from 0 to 255, such as 1=#ef5350. Colors not listed keep their built-in values.
---@field scheme? string Whether the theme is 'light' or 'dark', which programs in the terminal can ask for. If empty, the terminal decides from how bright background is, and treats no background as dark.

---@class rex.terminal.size.result.desired.item
---@field client_id? string The client that requested this size.
---@field columns integer The requested width in columns.
---@field rows integer The requested height in rows.
---@field updated_at string When the server received the request, as an RFC 3339 timestamp.

---@class rex.terminal.size.result
---@field cell_height_px integer The height of one character cell in pixels.
---@field cell_width_px integer The width of one character cell in pixels.
---@field columns integer The terminal's width in columns.
---@field desired rex.terminal.size.result.desired.item[] The last size each client requested, the owner's included, newest first.
---@field owner? string The client that owns the block, whose size the terminal uses. Null if the block has no owner.
---@field rows integer The terminal's height in rows.

---The process that set the title, as title_changed reported it. Left out if no title is set, a reset cleared it, or the server couldn't tell.
---@class rex.terminal.title.result.owner
---@field name string The process's name, found the same way as name in the result of process. Compare it as well as pid, because the system reuses process IDs.
---@field pid integer The process ID.

---@class rex.terminal.title.result
---@field owner? rex.terminal.title.result.owner The process that set the title, as title_changed reported it. Left out if no title is set, a reset cleared it, or the server couldn't tell.
---@field title string The terminal's title, as a program last set it. Empty if no title is set.

---@class rex.terminal.write.args
---@field data string The text to type. It can hold escape sequences for keys that aren't text, such as \u001b[A for the up arrow.

---@class rex.terminal.write.result
---@field bytes_written integer The number of bytes queued, which is the length of data in UTF-8 bytes, not characters.

---Call a method on a block and return its result, or `nil` and an error
---message. `block_id` is filled in from the current block when it is left out.
---
---```lua
---local title = rex.block.call("com.superlogical.terminal", "title", {}).title
---```
---
---The result is typed per method. `args` is not, because the method name is
---the second argument — name its class on a local when you want completion:
---
---```lua
-----@type rex.terminal.list_dir.args
---local args = { path = "/tmp" }
---local dirs = rex.block.call("com.superlogical.terminal", "list_dir", args)
---```
---@param creator rex.Creator The full name of the kind of block.
---@param method string The method's name. `rex actions` lists the block methods.
---@param args? table
---@return table|nil result
---@return string|nil err
---@overload fun(creator: "com.superlogical.onboarding", method: "set_step", args: rex.onboarding.set_step.args): rex.onboarding.set_step.result|nil, string|nil # Move the walkthrough to another step. Emits step_changed when the step changes.
---@overload fun(creator: "com.superlogical.onboarding", method: "state", args?: table): rex.onboarding.set_step.result|nil, string|nil # Return the step the walkthrough is on.
---@overload fun(creator: "com.superlogical.terminal", method: "blob_read", args?: rex.terminal.blob_read.args): rex.terminal.blob_read.result|nil, string|nil # Read a result that was too large for one reply or event, such as the output of format with max_bytes set, or a selection_copied event without text. The server keeps each result for about five minutes.
---@overload fun(creator: "com.superlogical.terminal", method: "clear", args?: table): table|nil, string|nil # Clear the screen and scrollback, keeping the current line at the top. It changes nothing while a full-screen program, such as an editor, is running.
---@overload fun(creator: "com.superlogical.terminal", method: "clipboard_read", args?: rex.terminal.clipboard_read.args): rex.terminal.clipboard_read.result|nil, string|nil # Read text that a program in the terminal copied to the clipboard. The server keeps each copy for about a minute, so a client can read text too large to send in an event.
---@overload fun(creator: "com.superlogical.terminal", method: "format", args?: rex.terminal.format.args): rex.terminal.format.result|nil, string|nil # Get the screen and scrollback as plain text, HTML, or escape sequences.
---@overload fun(creator: "com.superlogical.terminal", method: "input_mode", args?: table): rex.terminal.input_mode.result|nil, string|nil # Get how the terminal handles typed input, such as whether it is echoed. On Windows the call fails with not_found.
---@overload fun(creator: "com.superlogical.terminal", method: "list_dir", args?: rex.terminal.list_dir.args): rex.terminal.list_dir.result|nil, string|nil # List the subdirectories of a directory, and optionally its files. A relative path or ~ is resolved the way a program in the terminal would resolve it.
---@overload fun(creator: "com.superlogical.terminal", method: "modes", args?: table): rex.terminal.modes.result|nil, string|nil # List the terminal modes that programs can turn on, such as the alternate screen, bracketed paste, and mouse reporting, with each mode's name, number, and whether it is on. It doesn't wake an idle terminal.
---@overload fun(creator: "com.superlogical.terminal", method: "process", args?: table): rex.terminal.process.result|nil, string|nil # Get the processes running in the terminal and how its program exited.
---@overload fun(creator: "com.superlogical.terminal", method: "program_status", args?: table): rex.terminal.program_status.result|nil, string|nil # List the status that programs in the terminal have reported. Programs report a status, such as working or blocked, with OSC 7501 escape sequences.
---@overload fun(creator: "com.superlogical.terminal", method: "pwd", args?: table): rex.terminal.pwd.result|nil, string|nil # Get the working directory that a program in the terminal last reported. To find where each process actually is, call process instead.
---@overload fun(creator: "com.superlogical.terminal", method: "reset", args?: table): table|nil, string|nil # Reset the terminal to its starting state and clear the screen and scrollback. The program keeps running, and the size and default colors stay the same.
---@overload fun(creator: "com.superlogical.terminal", method: "resize", args: rex.terminal.resize.args): table|nil, string|nil # Request a terminal size in columns and rows. It applies at once if the caller owns the block or no client does, and otherwise when the caller becomes the owner.
---@overload fun(creator: "com.superlogical.terminal", method: "set_theme", args?: rex.terminal.set_theme.args): table|nil, string|nil # Replace the terminal's default colors. As with resize, they apply at once if the caller owns the block or no client does, and otherwise when the caller becomes the owner.
---@overload fun(creator: "com.superlogical.terminal", method: "size", args?: table): rex.terminal.size.result|nil, string|nil # Get the terminal's size, its owner, and the size each client last requested.
---@overload fun(creator: "com.superlogical.terminal", method: "title", args?: table): rex.terminal.title.result|nil, string|nil # Get the terminal's title and the process that set it.
---@overload fun(creator: "com.superlogical.terminal", method: "write", args: rex.terminal.write.args): rex.terminal.write.result|nil, string|nil # Type text into the terminal as if the user had typed it. The call returns once the text is queued, without waiting for the program to read it.
function BlockAPI.call(creator, method, args) end

---@class rex.action.client.appearance.switch_mode.args
---@field mode "light"|"dark"|"toggle" Use light or dark appearance, or toggle the currently visible appearance.

---@class rex.action.client.mode.enter.args
---@field name string The mode to enter, as the configuration names it.
---@field once? boolean Leave the mode after one of its own keys performs. The default is false.

---@class rex.action.client.tab.goto.args
---@field index integer The tab to show. Tabs count from 1, and -1 means the last tab.

---@class rex.action.client.tab.move.args
---@field direction "backward"|"forward"

---@class rex.action.client.theme.change.args
---@field name? string Theme name or ID to fuzzy-match. Omit to open the theme picker.

---@class rex.action.client.theme.import.args
---@field path string Absolute path of a Ghostty theme or iTerm color file on this machine.

---@class rex.action.com.superlogical.onboarding.set_step.args
---@field step string The step to move to. Empty means the first step.

---@class rex.action.com.superlogical.terminal.blob_read.args
---@field id? integer The result to read, from the reply or event that named it, such as the id in a format reply or a selection_copied event. Zero or left out reads the newest result, whatever produced it. If the server no longer has the result, the call fails with not_found.
---@field max_bytes? integer The most bytes of data to return, measured once it is encoded as a JSON string. Zero or left out means 49152 (48 KiB), and values above 983040 (960 KiB) are lowered to that. Must not be negative.
---@field offset? integer Where to start reading, in bytes from the start of the result. To read the next part, add the length in bytes of the data you received. Must not be negative.

---@class rex.action.com.superlogical.terminal.clipboard_read.args
---@field id? integer The copy to read, from a clipboard_written event or an earlier reply. Zero or left out reads the newest copy. If the server no longer has the copy, the call fails with not_found.
---@field max_bytes? integer The most bytes of data to return, measured once it is encoded as a JSON string. Zero or left out means 49152 (48 KiB), and values above 983040 (960 KiB) are lowered to that. Must not be negative.
---@field offset? integer Where to start reading, in bytes from the start of the text. To read the next part, add the length in bytes of the data you received. Must not be negative.

---@class rex.action.com.superlogical.terminal.format.args
---@field extra_charsets? boolean Include the selected character sets. Only used with the vt format.
---@field extra_cursor? boolean Include the cursor position. Only used with the vt format.
---@field extra_hyperlink? boolean Include the hyperlink in effect at the cursor. Only used with the vt format.
---@field extra_keyboard? boolean Include keyboard modes such as modifyOtherKeys. Only used with the vt format.
---@field extra_kitty_keyboard? boolean Include the state of the Kitty keyboard protocol. Only used with the vt format.
---@field extra_modes? boolean Include terminal modes that differ from their defaults. Only used with the vt format.
---@field extra_palette? boolean Include the color palette. Only used with the vt format.
---@field extra_protection? boolean Include the character protection mode. Only used with the vt format.
---@field extra_pwd? boolean Include the reported working directory. Only used with the vt format.
---@field extra_scrolling_region? boolean Include the scrolling region. Only used with the vt format.
---@field extra_style? boolean Include the text style in effect at the cursor. Only used with the vt format.
---@field extra_tabstops? boolean Include the tab stops. Only used with the vt format.
---@field format? string The format to return: 'text' for plain text, 'html' for HTML with inline styles, or 'vt' for escape sequences that redraw the screen with its colors and styles. Defaults to 'text'.
---@field max_bytes? integer The most bytes of content to return, measured once it is encoded as a JSON string. If more output remains, the reply has an id, and you read the rest with blob_read. Values above 983040 (960 KiB) are lowered to that. Zero or left out returns the whole output in one reply, and the call fails with too_large if the output doesn't fit. Must not be negative.
---@field trim? boolean Remove spaces from the end of each line.
---@field unwrap? boolean Join lines that the terminal wrapped because they were too long, so each line of output is one line the program wrote.

---@class rex.action.com.superlogical.terminal.list_dir.args
---@field files? boolean Include entries that aren't directories, such as files, and mark each with file set to true.
---@field limit? integer The most entries to return. Zero, negative, or left out means 2000.
---@field path? string The directory to list: an absolute path, ~ or a path under ~/ for the home directory, or a path relative to the working directory of the terminal's foreground process. Empty lists that working directory.
---@field prefix? string Keep only entries whose names start with this text, ignoring case. Use it to narrow a listing that came back truncated.

---@class rex.action.com.superlogical.terminal.resize.args
---@field cell_height_px? integer The height of one character cell in pixels, from 1 to 65535. Send it together with cell_width_px, or leave both out to keep the current cell size.
---@field cell_width_px? integer The width of one character cell in pixels, from 1 to 65535. Send it together with cell_height_px, or leave both out to keep the current cell size.
---@field columns integer The width in columns, that is, characters per line, from 1 to 65535.
---@field rows integer The height in rows, from 1 to 65535.

---@class rex.action.com.superlogical.terminal.set_theme.args
---@field background? string The default background color, as hex RRGGBB or #RRGGBB, such as #011627. Empty or left out uses the built-in color.
---@field cursor? string The cursor color, as hex RRGGBB or #RRGGBB, such as #80a4c2. Empty or left out uses the built-in color.
---@field foreground? string The default text color, as hex RRGGBB or #RRGGBB, such as #d6deeb. Empty or left out uses the built-in color.
---@field palette? string[] Changes to the 256-color palette, each written as index=RRGGBB or index=#RRGGBB with an index from 0 to 255, such as 1=#ef5350. Colors not listed keep their built-in values.
---@field scheme? string Whether the theme is 'light' or 'dark', which programs in the terminal can ask for. If empty, the terminal decides from how bright background is, and treats no background as dark.

---@class rex.action.com.superlogical.terminal.write.args
---@field data string The text to type. It can hold escape sequences for keys that aren't text, such as \u001b[A for the up arrow.

---@class rex.action.pane.focus.args
---@field direction "left"|"right"|"up"|"down"
---@field wrap? boolean Continue from the opposite edge of the layout when no pane lies in the direction. The default is true.

---@class rex.action.pane.move.args
---@field direction "left"|"right"|"up"|"down"

---@class rex.action.pane.resize.args
---@field amount? number How far to move the divider, as a percentage of the split it divides. The default is 5.
---@field direction "left"|"right"|"up"|"down"

---@class rex.action.pane.send_key.args
---@field key string The key to send, written the way a key binding is, such as "ctrl+u".

---@class rex.action.pane.split.args
---@field direction "right"|"down"|"left"|"up"|"auto"

---@class rex.action.session.collapse.args
---@field session_id? string The identifier that the session's server assigned. If omitted, the selected session.

---@class rex.action.session.collapse_others.args
---@field session_id? string The identifier that the session's server assigned. If omitted, the selected session.

---@class rex.action.session.expand.args
---@field session_id? string The identifier that the session's server assigned. If omitted, the selected session.

---@class rex.action.session.select.args
---@field session_id string The identifier that the session's server assigned.
---@field window_id? string A window in the session to show. If omitted, the session opens as usual.

---@class rex.api.block.close.args
---@field block_id? string The block to close. It may be a detached block. Send this or window_id, not both.
---@field keep_last? boolean If true, fail rather than close the only block left in a window, counting floating layers. Ignored for detached blocks.
---@field session_id string The session that holds the block.
---@field window_id? string The window whose focused block to close. It doesn't have to be the active window. Send this or block_id, not both.

---@class rex.api.block.close.result
---@field block_id string The block that closed.
---@field revision integer The session's revision after this call. It is unchanged if the block was detached, because a detached block isn't part of the view.

---The event to send: its type, such as paste, and its payload.
---@class rex.api.block.event.args.event
---@field payload? any The event's fields. Leaving it out is the same as an empty object.
---@field type string The kind of event, such as key, paste, mouse, wheel, or focus.

---@class rex.api.block.event.args
---@field block_id string The block to send the event to.
---@field event rex.api.block.event.args.event The event to send: its type, such as paste, and its payload.
---@field session_id string The session that holds the block.

---@class rex.api.block.method.args
---@field args? any The block method's own arguments. Leaving it out is the same as an empty object.
---@field block_id string The block to call the method on.
---@field method string The block method's short name, such as write or format.
---@field session_id string The session that holds the block.

---The methods and events that this creator's blocks provide.
---@class rex.api.block_creator.list.result.creators.item.api
---@field events? table The block events, by name. Each value is a JSON Schema for the event's fields.
---@field methods? table The block methods, by short name.

---@class rex.api.block_creator.list.result.creators.item.flavors.item
---@field description string A sentence about what this flavor creates.
---@field flavor string The flavor's full name, such as com.superlogical.terminal.shell. Use it in the flavor field of session.new_block or a layout.
---@field name string The flavor's short name within its creator, such as shell.
---@field options_schema? any A JSON Schema for the options this flavor accepts.
---@field title string A short name for people to read, such as Shell.

---@class rex.api.block_creator.list.result.creators.item
---@field api rex.api.block_creator.list.result.creators.item.api The methods and events that this creator's blocks provide.
---@field description string A sentence about what the creator's blocks do.
---@field features? string[] Optional features the creator supports. Ignore names you don't know. No names are defined yet.
---@field flavors? rex.api.block_creator.list.result.creators.item.flavors.item[] The flavors this creator offers.
---@field host_id? string Identifies the machine and account its blocks run as. If it equals your local server's host_id, its blocks see the same files you do.
---@field name string The creator's name, such as com.superlogical.terminal.
---@field title string A short name for people to read, such as Terminal.

---@class rex.api.block_creator.list.result
---@field creators? rex.api.block_creator.list.result.creators.item[] Every creator available, with its flavors and the API its blocks provide.

---@class rex.api.client.action.args.servers.item
---@field id string Any non-empty value the client uses to recognize the server. It comes back unchanged in client_call_requested events.
---@field name string The name an action uses for the server, in rex.server(name) and ctx.server. It must be unique and not empty.

---@class rex.api.client.action.args
---@field action string The full name of the action, such as dev_layout or com.superlogical.terminal.reset. client.keymap lists them.
---@field args? any The action's arguments, as a JSON object. Leave it out if there are none, and always for an action a creator declares.
---@field block_id? string The block the action applies to. Defaults to the session's focused block, unless server is set. Requires session_id.
---@field origin? string What caused the request: key, palette, or api (the default). The action reads it as ctx.origin. Other values count as api, but script makes the call fail with bad_request.
---@field server? string The name, from servers, of the server that holds session_id and block_id. Leave it out when they're on the server you're sending this to.
---@field servers? rex.api.client.action.args.servers.item[] Other servers this client is connected to, which a configuration file action can call with rex.server(name). Don't list the server you're sending this to.
---@field session_id? string The session the action applies to. Required for a block action. A configuration file action may leave it out, and then has no default session.

---@class rex.api.client.action.result.client_actions.item
---@field action string The name of the client action to perform. Ignore a name you don't know.
---@field args? any The action's arguments, as a JSON object. Left out if there are none.

---@class rex.api.client.action.result
---@field client_actions? rex.api.client.action.result.client_actions.item[] Actions the configuration file action asked this client to perform with rex.client.queue, in order. Perform each one as you would a key binding.
---@field ordered_after? string The id of the request this connection ran just before this one, counting only methods that run in order, such as client.key. Left out if there was none.
---@field result? any The block method's result, or what the configuration file action returned. Left out if that action returned nothing, nil, or true.

---@class rex.api.client.inspect.args
---@field client_id string The client to describe. It can be any client, not only the one calling.

---@class rex.api.client.inspect.result.actions.item
---@field args? any A JSON Schema of the arguments the action takes. Left out when the action doesn't describe any.
---@field category? string A group of related actions, such as Layouts. Configuration file actions without one are filed under Actions.
---@field description? string A sentence about what the action does, to show under its title in a menu or command palette.
---@field keywords? string More words to search for the action by, separated by spaces.
---@field kind? string What performs the action: client (the client itself), lua (a rex.action in the configuration file), or block (a block of the creator that the name starts with).
---@field name string The name that bindings use for the action, such as pane.split. Names from the configuration file never contain a dot.
---@field repeats? boolean True if holding the key down should run the action again, as holding an arrow key does.
---@field source? string Where the action is defined, as file:line, such as init.lua:12. Left out when there's no location, as for every action a client declares.
---@field title? string The name to show a person, such as Split Pane.

---The client, as client.list describes it.
---@class rex.api.client.inspect.result.client
---@field client_id string The client's ID. It stays the same when the client reconnects.
---@field connected_at string When the client first connected, as an RFC 3339 timestamp. It doesn't change when the client reconnects.
---@field connection_lost_at? string When the server noticed the connection drop, as an RFC 3339 timestamp. Set only while connection_state is lost.
---@field connection_state? string Whether the client has a connection: connected, or lost if it dropped and the client may still reconnect. Treat a missing or unknown value as connected.
---@field info? rex.session.list_clients.result.clients.item.info What the client says about itself, such as its name and version, for showing to people. Left out if it said nothing.
---@field principal rex.session.list_clients.result.clients.item.principal Who the client is, as the server identified it when it accepted the connection.
---@field session_ids? string[] The sessions the client attached to with session.attach or session.bootstrap, sorted by ID. Left out if there are none.

---@class rex.api.client.inspect.result
---@field actions? rex.api.client.inspect.result.actions.item[] The actions the client declared in its latest client.keymap request that listed any. Left out if it declared none.
---@field client rex.api.client.inspect.result.client The client, as client.list describes it.

---The key event, as the keyboard reported it.
---@class rex.api.client.key.args.key
---@field action "press"|"release"|"repeat" What happened to the key: press, release, or repeat while it's held down.
---@field composing boolean True while the key is part of an unfinished composition, such as a dead key or an input method.
---@field consumed_mods string The modifiers the keyboard layout used to produce text, such as shift for a capital letter. Written like mods, which also includes them.
---@field key "unidentified"|"backquote"|"backslash"|"bracket_left"|"bracket_right"|"comma"|"digit_0"|"digit_1"|"digit_2"|"digit_3"|"digit_4"|"digit_5"|"digit_6"|"digit_7"|"digit_8"|"digit_9"|"equal"|"intl_backslash"|"intl_ro"|"intl_yen"|"key_a"|"key_b"|"key_c"|"key_d"|"key_e"|"key_f"|"key_g"|"key_h"|"key_i"|"key_j"|"key_k"|"key_l"|"key_m"|"key_n"|"key_o"|"key_p"|"key_q"|"key_r"|"key_s"|"key_t"|"key_u"|"key_v"|"key_w"|"key_x"|"key_y"|"key_z"|"minus"|"period"|"quote"|"semicolon"|"slash"|"alt_left"|"alt_right"|"backspace"|"caps_lock"|"context_menu"|"control_left"|"control_right"|"enter"|"meta_left"|"meta_right"|"shift_left"|"shift_right"|"space"|"tab"|"convert"|"kana_mode"|"non_convert"|"delete"|"end"|"help"|"home"|"insert"|"page_down"|"page_up"|"arrow_down"|"arrow_left"|"arrow_right"|"arrow_up"|"num_lock"|"numpad_0"|"numpad_1"|"numpad_2"|"numpad_3"|"numpad_4"|"numpad_5"|"numpad_6"|"numpad_7"|"numpad_8"|"numpad_9"|"numpad_add"|"numpad_backspace"|"numpad_clear"|"numpad_clear_entry"|"numpad_comma"|"numpad_decimal"|"numpad_divide"|"numpad_enter"|"numpad_equal"|"numpad_memory_add"|"numpad_memory_clear"|"numpad_memory_recall"|"numpad_memory_store"|"numpad_memory_subtract"|"numpad_multiply"|"numpad_paren_left"|"numpad_paren_right"|"numpad_subtract"|"numpad_separator"|"numpad_up"|"numpad_down"|"numpad_right"|"numpad_left"|"numpad_begin"|"numpad_home"|"numpad_end"|"numpad_insert"|"numpad_delete"|"numpad_page_up"|"numpad_page_down"|"escape"|"f1"|"f2"|"f3"|"f4"|"f5"|"f6"|"f7"|"f8"|"f9"|"f10"|"f11"|"f12"|"f13"|"f14"|"f15"|"f16"|"f17"|"f18"|"f19"|"f20"|"f21"|"f22"|"f23"|"f24"|"f25"|"fn"|"fn_lock"|"print_screen"|"scroll_lock"|"pause"|"browser_back"|"browser_favorites"|"browser_forward"|"browser_home"|"browser_refresh"|"browser_search"|"browser_stop"|"eject"|"launch_app_1"|"launch_app_2"|"launch_mail"|"media_play_pause"|"media_select"|"media_stop"|"media_track_next"|"media_track_previous"|"power"|"sleep"|"audio_volume_down"|"audio_volume_mute"|"audio_volume_up"|"wake_up"|"copy"|"cut"|"paste" The physical key, named after its W3C KeyboardEvent code in snake_case, such as key_a for KeyA, the A key on a US keyboard. It doesn't change with the keyboard layout. Any other key is unidentified.
---@field mods string The modifier keys held down, joined with + in this order: shift, ctrl, alt, super, caps_lock, num_lock, shift_side, ctrl_side, alt_side, super_side. On a Mac, super is the Command key. The lock names mean the lock is on, and a name ending in _side means the right-hand key of that pair is held. An empty string means none.
---@field text string The text the key typed. Empty for keys that type nothing, such as arrows and modifiers.
---@field unshifted_codepoint integer The Unicode code point the key types without shift, such as 110 for n. Bindings such as ctrl+n match on it, so they follow the keyboard layout. 0 if none.

---@class rex.api.client.key.args
---@field block_id? string The block to send the key to. Defaults to the session's focused block.
---@field key rex.api.client.key.args.key The key event, as the keyboard reported it.
---@field prefix? string[] The keys pressed before key when key ends a key sequence, in order. For ctrl+b then x, send x as key and ctrl+b here.
---@field session_id string The session the key is typed in.

---@class rex.api.client.key.result
---@field handled boolean True if a Lua function in the client's script or the configuration file took the key. No block received it then.

---@class rex.api.client.keymap.args.defaults.item.bindings.item
---@field action string The name of the action to run.
---@field args? any The arguments to run the action with, as a JSON object, exactly as the user wrote them. Left out if there are none.
---@field keys? string[] The keys that trigger the binding, such as cmd+k, or cmd+[KeyK] for a key by its position. A key sequence has one element per key, in the order pressed.
---@field mode? string The mode the binding belongs to, such as resize. The binding applies only while that mode is active. Left out for a binding outside every mode.
---@field source? string Where the binding came from: a layer name, such as default, or a file:line, such as init.lua:12. Ignored in a request.

---@class rex.api.client.keymap.args.defaults.item.removals.item
---@field action? string The one action to take off the keys, such as pane.split. Leave it out to clear the keys whatever is bound to them.
---@field keys? string[] The keys to clear, spelled like a binding's keys. Only the same spelling matches. Empty, with mode set, to remove that whole mode.
---@field mode? string The mode to remove the keys from. Leave it out for keys outside every mode. With empty keys, it removes the whole mode.

---@class rex.api.client.keymap.args.defaults.item
---@field bindings? rex.api.client.keymap.args.defaults.item.bindings.item[] What the layer binds, in the order it bound them. A later binding sits above an earlier one on the same key.
---@field name string The layer's name, such as default or settings, which its bindings report as their source. Unique in the request, with no colon.
---@field removals? rex.api.client.keymap.args.defaults.item.removals.item[] What the layer takes off the layers beneath it. Removals never touch the layer's own bindings.

---@class rex.api.client.keymap.args
---@field actions? rex.api.client.inspect.result.actions.item[] The actions the client performs itself. Leave it out to skip the checks for unknown actions and wrong arguments. The server keeps the list for client.inspect and client.perform.
---@field defaults? rex.api.client.keymap.args.defaults.item[] The client's built-in layers, lowest first. They sit beneath the configuration file. Problems found only in them aren't reported, because the user can't edit them.
---@field overrides? rex.api.client.keymap.args.defaults.item[] Layers of shortcuts a person recorded in the client, lowest first. They sit above the configuration file and this client's scripts. Problems in them come back as warnings.
---@field server_actions? string Which of the server's actions the reply lists: all (the default), configured (only the configuration file's), or none. An unknown value means all.

---The configuration file the keymap came from. Present even when the file doesn't exist, so you can tell the user where to create it.
---@class rex.api.client.keymap.result.config
---@field exists boolean True if the file existed when the server last read it.
---@field generation integer How many times the server has loaded its configuration file since it started, counting from 1. It goes up even when the file didn't change, so compare hash and instance_id, not this, to tell whether a keymap you kept is current.
---@field hash string The SHA-256 of the configuration file and the modules it requires while it loads, in lowercase hexadecimal. Empty if there's no file or it couldn't be read.
---@field instance_id string The ID of the server process, as server.status reports it. A new value means the server restarted, so send your client.set_script scripts again.
---@field path string The absolute path of the configuration file, whether or not it exists.

---@class rex.api.client.keymap.result.diagnostics.item
---@field action? string The full name of the action the problem is about, as written, even if misspelled. Left out if it isn't about one action.
---@field key? string The key the problem is about, spelled like a binding's keys, with a sequence joined by spaces, such as ctrl+a b. Left out if it isn't about one key.
---@field message string The problem in one sentence, ready to show. Its wording may change, so don't parse it.
---@field mode? string The mode the problem is about. Left out if it isn't about a mode.
---@field severity string How serious the problem is: warning, for something the user probably didn't mean, or error, for a part that was skipped. Show a value you don't know as a warning.
---@field source? string Where the problem is, as file:line, such as init.lua:22. Left out when it isn't known.

---@class rex.api.client.keymap.result.modes.item
---@field blocked? string[][] Keys that must not reach the bindings outside the mode while it's active. If the mode also binds one, that binding still runs. Each is spelled like a binding's keys.
---@field exclusive boolean True if keys the mode doesn't bind are ignored while it's active. If false, they do what they do outside the mode.
---@field name string The mode's name, such as resize. Its bindings have it as their mode, and client.mode.enter takes it as its name argument.

---@class rex.api.client.keymap.result
---@field actions? rex.api.client.inspect.result.actions.item[] The server's actions that keys can be bound to: the configuration file's, then each creator's, by creator name. Left out when there are none.
---@field bindings? rex.api.client.keymap.args.defaults.item.bindings.item[] The resolved bindings, in the order to try them. When several share a key, run the first one whose action is available.
---@field config rex.api.client.keymap.result.config The configuration file the keymap came from. Present even when the file doesn't exist, so you can tell the user where to create it.
---@field diagnostics? rex.api.client.keymap.result.diagnostics.item[] Problems to show the user, such as a binding to an action that doesn't exist. Empty when there are none.
---@field modes? rex.api.client.keymap.result.modes.item[] Every mode in the keymap, sorted by name. Left out when there are none. The client decides which mode is active.

---@class rex.api.client.list.args
---@field kinds? string[] List only the clients of these kinds, such as app. A client that didn't report a kind matches none.
---@field session_id? string List only the clients attached to this session with session.attach or session.bootstrap.

---@class rex.api.client.list.result.clients.item
---@field client_id string The client's ID. It stays the same when the client reconnects.
---@field connected_at string When the client first connected, as an RFC 3339 timestamp. It doesn't change when the client reconnects.
---@field connection_lost_at? string When the server noticed the connection drop, as an RFC 3339 timestamp. Set only while connection_state is lost.
---@field connection_state? string Whether the client has a connection: connected, or lost if it dropped and the client may still reconnect. Treat a missing or unknown value as connected.
---@field info? rex.session.list_clients.result.clients.item.info What the client says about itself, such as its name and version, for showing to people. Left out if it said nothing.
---@field principal rex.session.list_clients.result.clients.item.principal Who the client is, as the server identified it when it accepted the connection.
---@field session_ids? string[] The sessions the client attached to with session.attach or session.bootstrap, sorted by ID. Left out if there are none.

---@class rex.api.client.list.result
---@field clients? rex.api.client.list.result.clients.item[] The clients, in the order they first connected. An empty list, never null, when none match.

---@class rex.api.client.perform.args
---@field action string The name of an action the client declared, such as pane.split. client.inspect lists them.
---@field args? any The action's arguments, as a JSON object. Leave it out if there are none.
---@field block_id? string The block to act on instead of the client's focused block, if any. The client receives it without the server checking it.
---@field client_id string The client to ask. To find one by its label, call target.resolve with kinds set to client.
---@field origin? string What caused the request, such as key, palette, api, or cli. The client receives it unchanged. Empty means api.
---@field session_id? string The session the action applies to, if any. The client receives it without the server checking it.
---@field timeout_ms? integer How long to wait for the answer, in milliseconds, once the client is sent the request. Defaults to 10000. Values over 30000 count as 30000.

---@class rex.api.client.perform.result
---@field performed_by string The client that performed the action. It is always the client that client_id named.
---@field result? any What the client's action returned. Left out if it returned null, as most actions do.

---Why the call failed or the action wasn't performed. Set this or result, not both.
---@class rex.api.client.reply.args.error
---@field code string The kind of failure, as an error code such as not_found, refused, or internal.
---@field message string What went wrong, written for people.

---@class rex.api.client.reply.args
---@field call_id string The call_id of the event you're answering.
---@field error? rex.api.client.reply.args.error Why the call failed or the action wasn't performed. Set this or result, not both.
---@field result? any The call's result, or what the action returned. Use null if there is none. Set this or error, not both.

---@class rex.api.client.set_label.args
---@field label string The label to set, such as studio. An empty string removes it. At most 64 bytes, with no control characters.

---@class rex.api.client.set_script.args.scripts.item
---@field name? string A file name to show in error messages and diagnostics, such as init.lua.
---@field source string The Lua code.

---@class rex.api.client.set_script.args
---@field scripts? rex.api.client.set_script.args.scripts.item[] Every Lua source to load, run in order. They replace all the scripts loaded before. An empty list unloads them.

---@class rex.api.client.set_script.result
---@field all_keys? boolean Never sent by this server. If an older server sends true, send every key to client.key, not only those in bindings.
---@field bindings? string[] The single keys the scripts bind to Lua functions. A key sent alone reaches the scripts only if it's listed here and pressed or repeating. Key sequences aren't listed.
---@field diagnostics? rex.api.client.keymap.result.diagnostics.item[] Problems in the scripts, written to show the user. The server skips each line it can't carry out and loads the rest.

---@class rex.api.data.connect.args
---@field block_id string The block that provides the data.
---@field data_ref_id string Which of the block's data sources to open, such as terminal.
---@field session_id string The session that holds the block. The connection must be attached to it with session.attach or session.bootstrap.
---@field subprotocol? string The subprotocol the data connection will use, such as rex.data.terminal.v1. If set, the ticket works only on a connection that uses it.

---@class rex.api.data.connect.result
---@field expires_in_ms integer How long the ticket stays valid after the server issues it, in milliseconds.
---@field ticket string A single-use ticket to send in the HELLO message that opens the data connection. Only a data connection from the same user can use it.

---@class rex.api.hosts.list.result.hosts.item
---@field added_from? string The device or client that added the host. For display only.
---@field endpoints? string[] The addresses that reach the host, in the order to try them, such as studio.tail1234.ts.net or unix:///tmp/rex.sock. Each takes a form the -S option of rex accepts. A socket path works only on a device where the path exists, such as the local end of an ssh -L forward.
---@field host_id? string The host_id that the host's server.status reported when the host was added, if known. Compare it with a server's host_id to tell whether this host is that server.
---@field icon? string The name of an icon for clients to show next to the label. Rex doesn't interpret it.
---@field id? string The host's ID, such as host:<uuid>, chosen by the device that adds the host. If hosts.put leaves it out, the server creates one.
---@field label string The name you connect to the host by, such as studio. Unique in the list, ignoring case, and at most 64 characters.
---@field source? string Where the configuration file declares the host, such as init.lua:12. Left out for a host added with hosts.put. The server sets it.
---@field updated_at? string When the host last changed, as an RFC 3339 timestamp. The server sets it and ignores any value a client sends.

---@class rex.api.hosts.list.result
---@field diagnostics? rex.api.client.keymap.result.diagnostics.item[] Warnings about the hosts declared in the configuration file. Left out when there are none. The hash doesn't cover them.
---@field hash string Identifies this version of the list. hosts.put, hosts.remove, and the hosts_changed event report the same hash for the same list.
---@field hosts? rex.api.hosts.list.result.hosts.item[] Every host in the list, sorted by label, ignoring case, then by id. An empty list when there are no hosts.

---The host to add, or to store in place of the host with the same id.
---@class rex.api.hosts.put.args.host
---@field added_from? string The device or client that added the host. For display only.
---@field endpoints? string[] The addresses that reach the host, in the order to try them, such as studio.tail1234.ts.net or unix:///tmp/rex.sock. Each takes a form the -S option of rex accepts. A socket path works only on a device where the path exists, such as the local end of an ssh -L forward.
---@field host_id? string The host_id that the host's server.status reported when the host was added, if known. Compare it with a server's host_id to tell whether this host is that server.
---@field icon? string The name of an icon for clients to show next to the label. Rex doesn't interpret it.
---@field id? string The host's ID, such as host:<uuid>, chosen by the device that adds the host. If hosts.put leaves it out, the server creates one.
---@field label string The name you connect to the host by, such as studio. Unique in the list, ignoring case, and at most 64 characters.
---@field source? string Where the configuration file declares the host, such as init.lua:12. Left out for a host added with hosts.put. The server sets it.
---@field updated_at? string When the host last changed, as an RFC 3339 timestamp. The server sets it and ignores any value a client sends.

---@class rex.api.hosts.put.args
---@field host rex.api.hosts.put.args.host The host to add, or to store in place of the host with the same id.

---The host as the server stored it, with its id and updated_at.
---@class rex.api.hosts.put.result.host
---@field added_from? string The device or client that added the host. For display only.
---@field endpoints? string[] The addresses that reach the host, in the order to try them, such as studio.tail1234.ts.net or unix:///tmp/rex.sock. Each takes a form the -S option of rex accepts. A socket path works only on a device where the path exists, such as the local end of an ssh -L forward.
---@field host_id? string The host_id that the host's server.status reported when the host was added, if known. Compare it with a server's host_id to tell whether this host is that server.
---@field icon? string The name of an icon for clients to show next to the label. Rex doesn't interpret it.
---@field id? string The host's ID, such as host:<uuid>, chosen by the device that adds the host. If hosts.put leaves it out, the server creates one.
---@field label string The name you connect to the host by, such as studio. Unique in the list, ignoring case, and at most 64 characters.
---@field source? string Where the configuration file declares the host, such as init.lua:12. Left out for a host added with hosts.put. The server sets it.
---@field updated_at? string When the host last changed, as an RFC 3339 timestamp. The server sets it and ignores any value a client sends.

---@class rex.api.hosts.put.result
---@field hash string Identifies the list after the call, the same way hosts.list does.
---@field host rex.api.hosts.put.result.host The host as the server stored it, with its id and updated_at.

---@class rex.api.hosts.remove.args
---@field id string The host to remove.

---@class rex.api.hosts.remove.result
---@field hash string Identifies the list after the call, the same way hosts.list does.
---@field removed boolean True if the host was in the list before the call and isn't now. False if there was nothing to remove.

---@class rex.api.server.apple.grant_file_access.args.files.item
---@field path string The file's absolute path. If it names a directory, access covers everything inside it.
---@field token? string A macOS sandbox extension token for path, which the app gets from sandbox_extension_issue_file. The server uses it only if it can't already open path.

---@class rex.api.server.apple.grant_file_access.args
---@field files? rex.api.server.apple.grant_file_access.args.files.item[] The files to make readable, in the order they were dropped or pasted. At most 256.

---@class rex.api.server.apple.grant_file_access.result.files.item
---@field access string 'readable' if the server could already open the file, 'granted' if it can now because it used the token, or 'denied' if it still can't. Treat a value you don't know as 'denied'.
---@field message? string Why access was denied, written for a log. The wording can change, so check access instead.
---@field path string The requested path, unchanged.

---@class rex.api.server.apple.grant_file_access.result
---@field files? rex.api.server.apple.grant_file_access.result.files.item[] One result for each requested file, in the same order as the request.

---@class rex.api.server.apple.privacy_status.result.services.item
---@field service string The protected resource. Later versions of Rex may add services, so skip one you don't know.
---@field status string 'granted' if the server may use the resource, 'denied' if the person turned it off or has not turned it on, 'not_determined' if the person has not been asked yet, or 'unknown' if the server could not tell. Treat a value you don't know as 'unknown'.

---@class rex.api.server.apple.privacy_status.result
---@field responsible_path? string The executable macOS holds responsible for the server. Privacy decisions belong to the app that contains it. Left out when the server can't tell.
---@field services? rex.api.server.apple.privacy_status.result.services.item[] One entry for each privacy service the server knows. Empty when the server can't read privacy decisions, for example when it doesn't run on macOS.

---@class rex.api.server.config_reload.result
---@field diagnostics? rex.api.client.keymap.result.diagnostics.item[] Problems found in the configuration file, written for people to read. Empty when there are none.
---@field generation integer How many times the server has loaded its configuration file since it started, counting this load. It goes up on every load, even one that fails.

---@class rex.api.server.status.result
---@field api_min_revision? integer The oldest revision of the API the server supports. Left out when it is 0.
---@field api_revision? integer The newest revision of the API the server supports. Left out when it is 0.
---@field config rex.session.bootstrap.result.server_status.config The configuration file the server loaded most recently. Always present, even when there is no file.
---@field exit_empty boolean True if the server stops on its own soon after its last session and last connection close.
---@field host_id? string Identifies the machine and account the server runs as. Servers that see the same files report the same value. Left out when the server has no state directory.
---@field instance_id string A random ID for this run of the server. A new value means a new server process, even if pid is the same.
---@field pid integer The server's process ID.
---@field session_count integer How many sessions the server has.
---@field started_at string When the server started, as an RFC 3339 timestamp.
---@field tailscale? rex.session.bootstrap.result.server_status.tailscale The status of built-in Tailscale. Present only when the server can run it.

---@class rex.api.server.stop.args
---@field if_idle? boolean Stop only if the server has no sessions and no other client is connected. A server that is in use refuses with conflict and keeps running.

---The status of built-in Tailscale after the call.
---@class rex.api.server.tailscale.disable.result.tailscale
---@field account? rex.session.bootstrap.result.server_status.tailscale.account The Tailscale account that signed the device in. Left out until Tailscale reports it.
---@field allow_tailnet_members boolean True if anyone your tailnet's access rules allow may connect. False lets in only the Tailscale user who signed the device in.
---@field auth_url? string The link to open to sign the device in to Tailscale. Present while state is needs_login, once Tailscale has made the link.
---@field dns_name? string The device's MagicDNS name, such as rex.tail1234.ts.net. Left out until known.
---@field error? string Why built-in Tailscale turned off after its HTTPS listener failed. The next enable clears it.
---@field https? rex.session.bootstrap.result.server_status.tailscale.https The state of the device's HTTPS listener. Left out while built-in Tailscale is off, and by older servers.
---@field state string 'disabled' (off), 'starting', 'needs_login' (see auth_url), 'needs_machine_approval' (a tailnet admin must approve the device), or 'running' (on the tailnet). Treat a value you don't know as 'starting'.
---@field tags? string[] The Tailscale tags the device has, such as tag:rex. A tagged device has no owner. Left out when it has none.
---@field tailnet_name? string The tailnet's display name, or its domain if it has none. Left out until known.

---@class rex.api.server.tailscale.disable.result
---@field tailscale rex.api.server.tailscale.disable.result.tailscale The status of built-in Tailscale after the call.

---@class rex.api.server.tailscale.enable.args
---@field advertise_tags? string[] Tailscale tags, such as tag:rex, for the device to sign in with. Used only when this call starts the device. Needs allow_tailnet_members set to true.
---@field allow_tailnet_members boolean True lets in anyone your tailnet's access rules allow. False lets in only the Tailscale user who signed the device in. Applies on every call, even when the device is already on.
---@field auth_key? string A Tailscale auth key that signs the device in without a browser. Used only when this call starts the device. Never logged or saved.
---@field hostname? string The device name to use if this call starts the device, such as studio. If left out, the server uses its configured name, or rex.

---The status of built-in Tailscale after the call. auth_url holds the sign-in link if it is ready.
---@class rex.api.server.tailscale.login.result.tailscale
---@field account? rex.session.bootstrap.result.server_status.tailscale.account The Tailscale account that signed the device in. Left out until Tailscale reports it.
---@field allow_tailnet_members boolean True if anyone your tailnet's access rules allow may connect. False lets in only the Tailscale user who signed the device in.
---@field auth_url? string The link to open to sign the device in to Tailscale. Present while state is needs_login, once Tailscale has made the link.
---@field dns_name? string The device's MagicDNS name, such as rex.tail1234.ts.net. Left out until known.
---@field error? string Why built-in Tailscale turned off after its HTTPS listener failed. The next enable clears it.
---@field https? rex.session.bootstrap.result.server_status.tailscale.https The state of the device's HTTPS listener. Left out while built-in Tailscale is off, and by older servers.
---@field state string 'disabled' (off), 'starting', 'needs_login' (see auth_url), 'needs_machine_approval' (a tailnet admin must approve the device), or 'running' (on the tailnet). Treat a value you don't know as 'starting'.
---@field tags? string[] The Tailscale tags the device has, such as tag:rex. A tagged device has no owner. Left out when it has none.
---@field tailnet_name? string The tailnet's display name, or its domain if it has none. Left out until known.

---@class rex.api.server.tailscale.login.result
---@field tailscale rex.api.server.tailscale.login.result.tailscale The status of built-in Tailscale after the call. auth_url holds the sign-in link if it is ready.

---@class rex.api.server.tailscale.rename.args
---@field hostname string The new device name, such as studio: 1 to 63 ASCII letters, digits, and hyphens, not starting or ending with a hyphen.

---The status of built-in Tailscale after the call. dns_name may still show the old name.
---@class rex.api.server.tailscale.rename.result.tailscale
---@field account? rex.session.bootstrap.result.server_status.tailscale.account The Tailscale account that signed the device in. Left out until Tailscale reports it.
---@field allow_tailnet_members boolean True if anyone your tailnet's access rules allow may connect. False lets in only the Tailscale user who signed the device in.
---@field auth_url? string The link to open to sign the device in to Tailscale. Present while state is needs_login, once Tailscale has made the link.
---@field dns_name? string The device's MagicDNS name, such as rex.tail1234.ts.net. Left out until known.
---@field error? string Why built-in Tailscale turned off after its HTTPS listener failed. The next enable clears it.
---@field https? rex.session.bootstrap.result.server_status.tailscale.https The state of the device's HTTPS listener. Left out while built-in Tailscale is off, and by older servers.
---@field state string 'disabled' (off), 'starting', 'needs_login' (see auth_url), 'needs_machine_approval' (a tailnet admin must approve the device), or 'running' (on the tailnet). Treat a value you don't know as 'starting'.
---@field tags? string[] The Tailscale tags the device has, such as tag:rex. A tagged device has no owner. Left out when it has none.
---@field tailnet_name? string The tailnet's display name, or its domain if it has none. Left out until known.

---@class rex.api.server.tailscale.rename.result
---@field tailscale rex.api.server.tailscale.rename.result.tailscale The status of built-in Tailscale after the call. dns_name may still show the old name.

---@class rex.api.server.version.result
---@field api_min_revision? integer The oldest revision of the API the server supports. Left out when it is 0.
---@field api_revision? integer The newest revision of the API the server supports. Left out when it is 0.
---@field built_at? string When the server was built, as an RFC 3339 timestamp. Most builds record the time of their Git commit. Left out when unknown.
---@field commit? string The Git commit the server was built from. Left out when unknown.
---@field dirty boolean True if the server was built from source with uncommitted changes.
---@field platform string The operating system and CPU the server was built for, such as darwin_arm64 or linux_amd64.
---@field rex_version string The version of Rex the server runs, as rex version prints it, such as 0.0.1780750825-g9a754d.

---How the server identified you when your connection opened.
---@class rex.api.server.whoami.result.principal
---@field display string A readable name for the client, such as uid 501 or alice@example.com. It can be out of date or shared, so compare uid, sid, or user_id instead.
---@field gid? integer The client's Unix group ID. Only for kind peer.
---@field kind string How the server identified the client: 'peer' (Unix socket credentials), 'windows_peer' (named pipe credentials), 'tailscale' (a Tailscale login), or 'anonymous' (not identified). Show a kind you don't know by its name.
---@field login_name? string The client's Tailscale login name, usually an email address. For display only.
---@field node? string The name of the client's Tailscale device. Only for kind tailscale.
---@field path? string[] The route the client took to this server, for troubleshooting. Entries include 'tailscale' (built-in Tailscale), 'tailnet' and 'tailnet-serve' (the machine's own Tailscale), and 'login-server' (passed on by a login server).
---@field pid? integer The client's process ID when it connected, for kind peer or windows_peer, if known. Process IDs are reused, so use it only for debugging.
---@field remote_addr? string The client's network address. Left out when there is none, such as on a Unix socket.
---@field sid? string The client's Windows security identifier, which survives account renames. Only for kind windows_peer.
---@field tags? string[] The Tailscale tags of the client's device. Any tag means the client is a machine, such as a CI runner, not a person.
---@field transport string How the client first reached Rex: 'unix', 'named_pipe', 'tcp', or 'tailscale'. A login server passes it on unchanged.
---@field uid? integer The client's Unix user ID. Only for kind peer.
---@field user_id? string The Tailscale user ID that owns the client's device. Only for kind tailscale. Check tags before treating it as a person.

---@class rex.api.server.whoami.result
---@field account? string The operating system account the server runs as. It can differ from your own name. Left out when unknown.
---@field principal rex.api.server.whoami.result.principal How the server identified you when your connection opened.

---@class rex.api.session.attach.args
---@field session_id string The session to attach to.

---@class rex.api.session.attach_all.args
---@field event_scope string Which events to receive. 'session' sends every session's events except block_event, plus session_created, session_destroyed, session_moved, and tailscale_status_changed. 'all' also sends block_event and the client events, such as client_joined.

---@class rex.api.session.bootstrap.args
---@field attach_session_ids? string[] The sessions to attach to, in order. Unknown and repeated IDs are skipped, and an empty list attaches none. Leave it out to let the server pick one.
---@field data_tickets? rex.session.bootstrap.args.data_tickets.item[] The kinds of block to issue data tickets for. The response has a ticket for each visible block in an attached session that matches one. At most 8 entries.
---@field event_scope string Which events to receive. 'session' sends every session's events except block_event, plus session_created, session_destroyed, session_moved, and tailscale_status_changed. 'all' also sends block_event and the client events, such as client_joined.
---@field preferred_session_id? string The session to attach to, if it exists. Otherwise the server attaches the first session. Ignored when attach_session_ids is set.

---@class rex.api.session.close_layer.args
---@field layer_id? string The floating layer to close. Every block in it closes too.
---@field session_id string The session to act on.

---@class rex.api.session.close_window.args
---@field session_id string The session to act on.
---@field window_id? string The window to close. Leave it out to close the active window. Every block in it closes too.

---@class rex.api.session.create.args
---@field initial_windows? rex.session.create.args.initial_windows.item[] Windows to create with the session, in order. The last one becomes the active window. If any of them fails, the server creates no session.
---@field label? string The session's label, up to 128 characters. Leave it out to let the server choose one, such as quiet-meadow.

---@class rex.api.session.describe_block.args
---@field block_id? string The block to describe. It may be a detached block.
---@field session_id string The session to act on.

---@class rex.api.session.destroy.args
---@field session_id string The session to destroy. Every block in it closes.

---@class rex.api.session.detach.args
---@field session_id string The session to detach from.

---@class rex.api.session.detach_block.args
---@field block_id? string The block to detach. It keeps running but leaves its window.
---@field session_id string The session to act on.

---@class rex.api.session.disown_block.args
---@field block_ids? string[] The blocks to give up. Leave it out or empty to give up every block in the session.
---@field session_id string The session to act on.

---@class rex.api.session.float_block.args
---@field block_id? string The block to float. It must be in its window's layout, not already in a floating layer.
---@field bounds any Where the new floating layer sits, as [x, y, w, h] fractions of the window's width and height. It must fit inside the window.
---@field session_id string The session to act on.

---@class rex.api.session.focus_block.args
---@field block_id? string The block to focus. It must be in a window, which becomes the active window.
---@field session_id string The session to act on.

---@class rex.api.session.focus_direction.args
---@field direction "left"|"right"|"up"|"down" Which way to move focus from the focused block in the active window.
---@field session_id string The session to act on.
---@field wrap? boolean Whether to continue from the opposite edge when no block lies that way. Defaults to false. Older servers ignore it and always wrap.

---@class rex.api.session.focus_next_pane.args
---@field session_id string The session to act on.
---@field wrap? boolean Whether to go from the last block back to the first. Defaults to false.

---@class rex.api.session.focus_next_window.args
---@field session_id string The session to act on.
---@field wrap? boolean Whether to go from the last window back to the first. Defaults to false.

---@class rex.api.session.focus_previous_pane.args
---@field session_id string The session to act on.
---@field wrap? boolean Whether to go from the first block back to the last. Defaults to false.

---@class rex.api.session.focus_previous_window.args
---@field session_id string The session to act on.
---@field wrap? boolean Whether to go from the first window back to the last. Defaults to false.

---@class rex.api.session.focus_window.args
---@field session_id string The session to act on.
---@field window_id? string The window to make active. Focus goes to the block that was focused in it.

---@class rex.api.session.help.args
---@field session_id string The session to act on.

---@class rex.api.session.list_blocks.args
---@field session_id string The session to act on.

---@class rex.api.session.list_clients.args
---@field session_id string The session to act on.

---@class rex.api.session.lower_layer.args
---@field layer_id? string The floating layer to move back.
---@field relative_layer_id? string Another floating layer in the same window to place it just below. Leave it out to move it behind every other floating layer.
---@field session_id string The session to act on.

---@class rex.api.session.move.args
---@field before_session_id? string The session to place it just before. Leave it out to move the session to the end.
---@field session_id string The session to move.

---@class rex.api.session.move_block.args
---@field anchor_block_id? string The block to place it beside. It must be placed in a window.
---@field block_id? string The block to move. It may be detached or placed in any window.
---@field direction? "horizontal"|"vertical" How to divide the anchor's space: 'horizontal' for side by side or 'vertical' for one above the other. Defaults to horizontal.
---@field ratio? number The fraction of the space that the before part gets, greater than 0 and less than 1. Defaults to 0.5.
---@field session_id string The session to act on.
---@field side? "before"|"after" Where the moved block goes: 'before' for the left or top part, or 'after' for the right or bottom part. Defaults to after.

---@class rex.api.session.move_window.args
---@field before_window_id? string The window to place it just before. Leave it out to move the window to the end.
---@field session_id string The session to act on.
---@field window_id? string The window to move.

---@class rex.api.session.new_block.args
---@field flavor string The flavor of block to create, such as com.superlogical.terminal.shell. block_creator.list lists them.
---@field label? string The block's label, up to 128 characters. Defaults to the flavor's short name, such as shell.
---@field options? any Settings for the block's creator, in the shape of the flavor's options_schema from block_creator.list.
---@field session_id string The session to act on.

---@class rex.api.session.new_layer.args
---@field bounds any Where the new floating layer sits, as [x, y, w, h] fractions of the window's width and height. It must fit inside the window.
---@field focus? boolean Whether to make the window active and focus the first new block. Defaults to false.
---@field layout? table The blocks to create in the floating layer and how to arrange them. Each node holds either block, with a flavor and optional options and label, or split, with direction, before, after, and an optional ratio.
---@field session_id string The session to act on.
---@field window_id? string The window to add the floating layer to. Leave it out to use the active window.

---@class rex.api.session.new_split.args
---@field anchor_id? string The block or split whose space to split. A block ID splits that one block. A split ID puts the new blocks beside that split and everything under it. Leave it out to split the focused block of window_id, or of the active window. Don't send it with window_id.
---@field direction "horizontal"|"vertical" How to divide the anchor's space: 'horizontal' for side by side or 'vertical' for one above the other.
---@field focus? boolean Whether to make the anchor's window active and focus the first new block. Defaults to false.
---@field layout? table The blocks to create beside the anchor and how to arrange them. Each node holds either block, with a flavor and optional options and label, or split, with direction, before, after, and an optional ratio.
---@field ratio? number The fraction of the space that the before part gets, greater than 0 and less than 1. Defaults to 0.5.
---@field session_id string The session to act on.
---@field side "before"|"after" Where the new blocks go: 'before' for the left or top part, or 'after' for the right or bottom part.
---@field window_id? string The window whose focused block to split. Send it only when anchor_id is left out.

---@class rex.api.session.new_window.args
---@field focus? boolean Whether to make the new window the active window. Defaults to true. A session with no active window always makes the new one active.
---@field layout? table The blocks to create in the window and how to arrange them. Required. Each node holds either block, with a flavor and optional options and label, or split, with direction, before, after, and an optional ratio.
---@field session_id string The session to act on.
---@field window_label? string The window's label, up to 128 characters. Leave it out for a label made from the window's ID, such as Window 89abcdef.

---@class rex.api.session.own_block.args
---@field block_ids? string[] The blocks to claim. Leave it out or empty to claim every block in the session.
---@field mode? string How to claim them. 'take' makes the calling client the owner right away. 'request' makes it the owner only of blocks that have none, and it may get the rest when their owner gives them up or leaves. Defaults to take.
---@field session_id string The session to act on.

---@class rex.api.session.raise_layer.args
---@field layer_id? string The floating layer to move forward.
---@field relative_layer_id? string Another floating layer in the same window to place it just above. Leave it out to move it in front of every other floating layer.
---@field session_id string The session to act on.

---@class rex.api.session.resize_layer.args
---@field bounds any The layer's new position and size, as [x, y, w, h] fractions of the window's width and height. It must fit inside the window.
---@field layer_id? string The floating layer to move or resize.
---@field session_id string The session to act on.

---@class rex.api.session.resize_pane.args
---@field after_block_id? string A block on the right of the border, or below it when direction is vertical.
---@field before_block_id? string A block on the left of the border, or above it when direction is vertical.
---@field direction "horizontal"|"vertical" The kind of split between the two blocks: 'horizontal' for a border between left and right, or 'vertical' for one between top and bottom.
---@field ratio number The fraction of the split's space that the before part gets, greater than 0 and less than 1.
---@field session_id string The session to act on.

---@class rex.api.session.set_block_label.args
---@field block_id? string The block to relabel. Required.
---@field label? string The new label, up to 128 characters. An empty or missing label restores a default made from the block's ID.
---@field session_id string The session to act on.

---@class rex.api.session.set_label.args
---@field label? string The new label, up to 128 characters. An empty or missing label restores the label the server chose when it created the session.
---@field session_id string The session to act on.

---@class rex.api.session.set_window_label.args
---@field label? string The new label, up to 128 characters. An empty or missing label restores a default made from the window's ID.
---@field session_id string The session to act on.
---@field window_id? string The window to relabel.

---@class rex.api.session.swap_blocks.args
---@field block_id? string One of the two blocks to swap.
---@field other_block_id? string The other block. The order of the two doesn't matter.
---@field session_id string The session to act on.

---@class rex.api.session.view.args
---@field session_id string The session to act on.

---@class rex.api.session.zoom_block.args
---@field block_id? string The block to zoom. It takes focus, and its window becomes active. Leave it out to use the focused block of window_id, or of the active window.
---@field session_id string The session to act on.
---@field window_id? string A window whose focused block to zoom. The active window doesn't change. Send it only when block_id is left out.
---@field zoom? boolean Set true to zoom or false to restore the layout. Leave it out to switch between the two.

---Where the caller is. Only session_id is used: without scope.session, windows and blocks come from that session.
---@class rex.api.target.complete.args.context
---@field block_id? string The caller's block, such as the terminal a script runs in. In target.resolve, empty block and window queries default to it and its window.
---@field client_id? string The caller's own client. In target.resolve, an empty client query selects it if it's among the clients searched.
---@field session_id? string The caller's session. Without scope.session, windows and blocks are searched only in this session, and an empty session query selects it.

---Limits completion to one session, or block completion to one window, as in target.resolve.
---@class rex.api.target.complete.args.scope
---@field session? string The session to search in, named by ID, label, or the end of its ID. A client search then covers only clients attached to it.
---@field window? string The window to search in, named by ID, label, position, or the end of its ID. Allowed only when kinds is just block.

---@class rex.api.target.complete.args
---@field context? rex.api.target.complete.args.context Where the caller is. Only session_id is used: without scope.session, windows and blocks come from that session.
---@field kinds? string[] What to complete: session, window, block, or client, with the same rules as in target.resolve. Completions come back in this order.
---@field prefix string The text typed so far. It matches the start of labels (ignoring case), window positions, and short IDs. An empty prefix matches everything.
---@field scope? rex.api.target.complete.args.scope Limits completion to one session, or block completion to one window, as in target.resolve.

---The session, window, block, or client that value names.
---@class rex.api.target.complete.result.completions.item.target
---@field block_id? string The block's ID. Set only for blocks.
---@field client_id? string The client's ID. Set only for clients.
---@field kind string What this is: session, window, block, or client. Skip a candidate whose kind you don't know.
---@field label string Its label, or an empty string if it has none.
---@field ordinal? integer A window's position in its session, counting from 1, in the order session.view lists windows. Left out for everything else.
---@field session_id? string The session's ID, or the ID of the session that holds the window or block. Left out for a client.
---@field where? string Where it is, for showing to a person, such as 'window 2' or 'detached'. For a client, the program, such as 'Rex.app 1.6.0'. May be empty. Don't parse it.
---@field window_id? string The window's ID, or the ID of the window that holds the block. Left out for sessions, clients, and detached blocks.

---@class rex.api.target.complete.result.completions.item
---@field form string What value is: 'label', 'ordinal' (a window position), or 'id' (a short ID, the last six hexadecimal digits of an ID).
---@field target rex.api.target.complete.result.completions.item.target The session, window, block, or client that value names.
---@field value string The text to offer, such as a label, a window position, or a short ID.

---@class rex.api.target.complete.result
---@field completions? rex.api.target.complete.result.completions.item[] Every match, grouped by kind in the order of kinds. Empty if nothing matches or a scope doesn't resolve.

---Where the caller is, such as the terminal a script runs in. IDs in it that no longer exist are ignored.
---@class rex.api.target.resolve.args.context
---@field block_id? string The caller's block, such as the terminal a script runs in. In target.resolve, empty block and window queries default to it and its window.
---@field client_id? string The caller's own client. In target.resolve, an empty client query selects it if it's among the clients searched.
---@field session_id? string The caller's session. Without scope.session, windows and blocks are searched only in this session, and an empty session query selects it.

---Limits the search to one session, or a block search to one window. Not allowed when kinds includes session.
---@class rex.api.target.resolve.args.scope
---@field session? string The session to search in, named by ID, label, or the end of its ID. A client search then covers only clients attached to it.
---@field window? string The window to search in, named by ID, label, position, or the end of its ID. Allowed only when kinds is just block.

---@class rex.api.target.resolve.args
---@field context? rex.api.target.resolve.args.context Where the caller is, such as the terminal a script runs in. IDs in it that no longer exist are ignored.
---@field kinds? string[] What each query may name: session, window, block, or client. List each kind once, and list client only on its own.
---@field queries? string[] The names to look up. Each is a full ID, a label, a window position, the end of an ID, or an empty string for the default.
---@field scope? rex.api.target.resolve.args.scope Limits the search to one session, or a block search to one window. Not allowed when kinds includes session.

---@class rex.api.target.resolve.result.results.item.candidates.item
---@field block_id? string The block's ID. Set only for blocks.
---@field client_id? string The client's ID. Set only for clients.
---@field kind string What this is: session, window, block, or client. Skip a candidate whose kind you don't know.
---@field label string Its label, or an empty string if it has none.
---@field ordinal? integer A window's position in its session, counting from 1, in the order session.view lists windows. Left out for everything else.
---@field session_id? string The session's ID, or the ID of the session that holds the window or block. Left out for a client.
---@field where? string Where it is, for showing to a person, such as 'window 2' or 'detached'. For a client, the program, such as 'Rex.app 1.6.0'. May be empty. Don't parse it.
---@field window_id? string The window's ID, or the ID of the window that holds the block. Left out for sessions, clients, and detached blocks.

---What the query matched. Set only when status is resolved.
---@class rex.api.target.resolve.result.results.item.target
---@field block_id? string The block's ID. Set only for blocks.
---@field client_id? string The client's ID. Set only for clients.
---@field kind string What this is: session, window, block, or client. Skip a candidate whose kind you don't know.
---@field label string Its label, or an empty string if it has none.
---@field ordinal? integer A window's position in its session, counting from 1, in the order session.view lists windows. Left out for everything else.
---@field session_id? string The session's ID, or the ID of the session that holds the window or block. Left out for a client.
---@field where? string Where it is, for showing to a person, such as 'window 2' or 'detached'. For a client, the program, such as 'Rex.app 1.6.0'. May be empty. Don't parse it.
---@field window_id? string The window's ID, or the ID of the window that holds the block. Left out for sessions, clients, and detached blocks.

---@class rex.api.target.resolve.result.results.item
---@field candidates? rex.api.target.resolve.result.results.item.candidates.item[] When status is ambiguous, the choices for a person to pick from. Check each one's kind.
---@field query string The query this result answers, exactly as sent.
---@field reason? string Why the query didn't resolve, for showing to a person. May be empty.
---@field status string Whether the query matched: 'resolved' for one thing, 'not_found' for nothing, or 'ambiguous' for several. Treat a value you don't know as 'not_found'.
---@field target? rex.api.target.resolve.result.results.item.target What the query matched. Set only when status is resolved.

---@class rex.api.target.resolve.result
---@field results? rex.api.target.resolve.result.results.item[] One result for each query, in the same order as queries.

---Call any server method by its full name, including the ones `rex.session`
---doesn't cover. Returns the result table, or `nil` and an error message — a
---failure never raises.
---
---```lua
---local clients, err = rex.call("client.list")
---```
